A||||17||Pontiaddict||Stupid Wheel Locks!||||8||
Z||000000||Pontiaddict||09-30-2000||11:41 AM||||Does anyone know how to get wheel locks off without a key? I have to get the front wheels off of my car so I can finish some suspension work. The locks I have are round with notches cut into the sides.<P>PS. My car has a 1.5" drop in the back now but not the front. So I need the info quickly.<P>||||reg||8
Z||000001||JohnnyK||09-30-2000||12:08 PM||||Uh oh.. Same problem I had. Your in for some real work if they are on there tight. Tried many things, the one that worked the best was banging a smaller socket of it (Make you sure bang it on there perfect.. You usually only get one shot) and then cranking it. I found if I used my impact socket, it just rounded it off. When that didn't work on one of my wheels, I had to alter the shape with a bunch of grinders (so that my socket would fit again.... Actually, didn't use grinders, used cut of wheels in hopes I could cut it off, but there wasn't enough room. Then, I torched it, and then I banged a smaller socket over it when it was still hot... Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-30-2000||02:17 PM||||See, there <B>are</B> a few good uses for those cheap $5 socket sets! ||||reg||3
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-30-2000||02:29 PM||||Take it down to your local tire store.  They should have a set of socket type things that are made to remove rounded off lug nuts and those pesky wheel locks.  At least that's what they used when I broke my adapter about a month ago.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||09-30-2000||02:46 PM||||I went through the same problem recently with those stupid wheel locks.  I had a flat tire and the wheel lock was rusted a little and I couldn't get the wheel lock on.  I had to hammer it on.  I eventually got it on and I went to the tire place to get new tires and an alignment.  They couldn't get the wheel lock off of the nut (I hammered it on too good) so they had a hell of a time getting the other three off.  Not too happy with me....<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||09-30-2000||04:36 PM||||<BR>Look at the bright side guys, at least these wheel locks appear to be doing their job well. There wouldn't be much point having them on in the first place if they came off easy (without the key). <P>In view of the problem with rust, it would be a good idea to put a little grease or oil on the wheel stud threads before both the regular and lock nuts are put on the wheels. This is probably even more important in climates where cars are driven on roads in the winter that have been salted.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||09-30-2000||05:31 PM||||I just don't understand why someone would steal those aluminum Pontiac rims..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||AkursedX||09-30-2000||05:38 PM||||Well if you have torches, get a lead pipe that will almost it over the lug.  Heat the pipe up real good, and then hammer it on to the lug, then let cool, and use a pipe wrench on the pipe to turn it off.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000008||Patrick||09-30-2000||06:40 PM||||<BR>A <B><I>lead</I></B> pipe?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Philphine||09-30-2000||07:34 PM||||i've done the hammer on smaller socket thing on two fieros. if you find the size but you break it or wear it out on the first wheel, go to a pawn shop and get a few more of that size for cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||09-30-2000||07:43 PM||||Call the deallers in your area. there is a master key set for those locks. I've see notes about who makes them on other sites. Try FOSG. I believe you can still order keys for these once you know what master key fits them.<P>If/When you replace them <A HREF="http://www.mcguard.com/" TARGET=_blank>McGard</A> makes better locks and they are available thru pep boys and other outlets. (You may have to order them) You can also order extra keys.||||reg||1
Z||000011||hrocker||10-01-2000||05:02 AM||||If all else fails especially with McGard style locks...you can tack weld a large nut on the lock lug nut and then turn it off with the appropriate socket.||||reg||14
Z||000012||theogre||10-01-2000||10:54 AM||||I missed this in th thread earlier....<P>You aren't suposed to put lubricant on the wheel studs at all, but I fully understand why it's done. I do it myself. It really is a nesesity in many areas because of weather and salt spray.<P>What you don't want is oil or grease on the lugs. Even when you put it on just the threads it can run or wick to other areas and let the lugs come loose. Not a good thing. (Some niny did this to my fathers car once. we almost lost a wheel.)<P>If you want to protect the threads put anti sieze on the threads and use only enough to get the job done. Antiseize won't run or wick like oil/grease can. It also doesn't easily wash off. so any area of thread covered with it is getting some protection<P>Make sure NONE of the anitsieze gets on the cone face of the lug or on the lug seat of the wheel. The cones are what lock the lugs so they can't come loose.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Patrick||10-01-2000||04:00 PM||||<BR><B>Ogre</B>, I did hesitate a bit before I posted about putting a <I>little</I> grease or oil on the stud threads, but possibly I should have emphasized the word <I>little</I> a <I>little</I> more. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> There'll always be someone out there who thinks that if a little is good then a whole lot must be better. I put no more than two drops of engine oil on each of the stud threads. I've never had a problem myself, but that doesn't mean that there's no possibility that someone else might have a problem such as you pointed out. I'm glad that you admitted that you do it yourself though. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pontiaddict||10-01-2000||04:25 PM||||I didn't get to reply yesterday.<BR>I tried the socket thing.... No good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I tried taking it to the tire shop (More like a service station that sells tires).... They didn't know anything about how to remove them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>The way my wheels are made (They are some old BBS wheels, not the Pontiac ones, JohnnyK) I can't get a grinder on the nuts without messing up the rims.<P>The parts store I usually go to is supposed to have a tool to take them off. Of course, I found this out too late to buy one and now I have to wait until Monday. <P>Thanks For the input.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||10-01-2000||07:19 PM||||Patrick, A little you can get away with. You're right, many people will do just what you said. I've used oil grease and antisieze. If you have a tube, and everyone should, anitsieze is best. What ever you use keep it off the cones.||||reg||1
Z||000016||roadrunner||10-01-2000||10:25 PM||||There is an easy sure fire way to remove the stock Pontiac wheel locks on our Fieros as long as the grooves are cut into the outside diameter of the wheel lock nut.  Get some hard wire such as a coat hanger about 0.080 inch in diameter and cut it into 1/2 inch lenghts.  Put some wheel bearing grease on the outside diameter of the wheel lock nut and insert a piece of wire in each groove.  Carefully put a 13/16 inch socket over the wires and lock nut and use a 1/2 inch draw bar to remove the nut.  I have done this a number of times. Works like a charm.  Better than the coat hanger wire are 1/2 inch long needle rollers from an old needle bearing or caged roller assembly. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||HugGRRRFiero||10-02-2000||08:47 AM||||Isn't there a kit you can buy for this situation.  I think it costs $50-75 bucks at a parts store.  A friend of mine told me about it a long time ago this happened to him and he told me about the kit.<P>If you've got access to a welder then you could try welding a junk socket to the lock and turn it off.<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">         <BR>         <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 10-02-2000).]||||reg||1
